53, -0.0068878345, 0.041804254, -0.021715278, -0.014019994, 0.016232658, 0.016917424, -0.005911451, -0.031385478, 0.015930414, 0.026321907, 0.006966349, -0.020400258, -0.016435033, 0.008558094, 0.01015215, 0.017996771, -0.0051278, -0.016344387, 0.013476311, 0.021807604, 0.0012992634, 0.0148979295, 0.018998636, 0.010078416, 0.00286494, -0.023350338, 0.008116829, 0.023457838, 0.0071163652, 0.020143354, -0.019562276, -0.0065515097, -0.014182546, 0.01795787, -0.011065905, 0.014276009, -0.03100101, -0.0055981576, 0.00061058125, 0.019671481, -0.0064352057, -0.008498685, 0.003370148, -0.0087643005, 0.006373965, 0.0025203174, -0.016281908, -0.007656332, 0.017646207, -0.00017504355, -0.026972186, -0.007534742, -0.018868824, 0.0033217769, -0.014687058, -0.0062408727, 4.8946076e-05, 8.336373e-06, -0.009477071]}}}, {'index': {'_index': 'chunks', '_id': 'chunk_116bfc76-d9ca-4164-b677-253e452eb49a_2', 'status': 400, 'error': {'type': 'mapper_parsing_exception', 'reason': "failed to parse field [embedding] of type [knn_vector] in document with id 'chunk_116bfc76-d9ca-4164-b677-253e452eb49a_2'. Preview of field's value: 'null'", 'caused_by': {'type': 'illegal_argument_exception', 'reason': 'Vector dimension mismatch. Expected: 768, Given: 3072'}}, 'data': {'source_type': <SourceType.UPLOADED_FILE: 'uploaded_file'>, 'source_id': '116bfc76-d9ca-4164-b677-253e452eb49a', 'source_name': 'Bloomberg QRT Article.pdf', 'content': '“They’re extremely curious, sensible, consistent and especially rigorous, I would say to\nthe nth degree,” says Bobby Jain, the French duo’s former boss at Credit Suisse who\nstarted his own hedge fund last year. “You think about engineering, Cartesian thinking,\nthis is the best of it.”\nMorlat and Laizet declined to comment. This story is based on interviews with more\nthan half a dozen people who know QRT or its founders. Most didn’t want to be\nidentified discussing private matters at a firm where secrecy reins. Morlat, 58, and\nLaizet, 53, avoid the media and have resisted hiring even a PR agency. When QRT\nmarketers talk to potential clients, they show a printed presentation that’s diligently\ntaken away afterward.\nCorporate Culture\nIn some ways QRT does still resemble the multistrat behemoths that it’s now rubbing\nshoulders with. It runs a “market-making” unit where it matches buyers and sellers in\nshares, using algorithms to shave off hundreds of millions of dollars of profit from\nmicroscopic price differences. It also trades physical commodities just like billionaire\nKen Griffin’s Citadel, and it invests in early-stage tech companies like a venture-capital\nfirm.\nAbout 45 other hedge funds run money for QRT — including mainstays such as\nDavidson Kempner Capital Management — making it one of the industry’s biggest\nbackers of external traders along with Millennium.\nWhere it’s unusual is in how it runs its own business. It doesn’t just hand pots of cash to\nlots of separate teams and largely leave them to it: rewarding those who do well, and\nsacking those who don’t. QRT is more centralized, with Chief Investment Officer Laizet\nhaving full discretion on how much risk to take and when. Its tech and collaboration\nbetween teams let it execute higher-quality trades, and thereby keep down costs. That’s\nlucrative when you’re operating on razor-thin margins at jumbo volumes.\nQRT’s setup points to how much the hedge fund world is changing from a protoype\npioneered by Alfred Winslow Jones more than 70 years ago. For a long time, firms have\nbeen centered around powerful founders, and latterly the superstar traders whom\nthey’ve entrusted to run client money. It’s still difficult to imagine Citadel without Griffin\nor Millennium without Izzy Englander.\nThat’s a harder sell these days. As the top hedge funds balloon in size, investors want\nleaders with both investing bona fides and the chops to run large, increasingly complex\norganizations. Corporate culture is all the rage.\n“Qube is not actually all about Pym,” says Stephen Dainton, president of Barclays Bank\nPlc who previously worked with him and Laizet at Credit Suisse. “Unlike a number of\nthese very talented single principals who build a structure around themselves and', 'start_pos': None, 'end_pos': None, 'bbox': {'page': 3, 'x': 0.0, 'y': 0.0, 'width': 595.32, 'height': 841.92, 'selector': None, 'xpath': None, 'offset': None, 'line_start': None, 'line_end': None, 'char_start': None, 'char_end': None}, 'metadata': {'page_number': 3, 'page_width': 595.32, 'page_height': 841.92, 'has_embedding': True}, 'id': 'chunk_116bfc76-d9ca-4164-b677-253e452eb49a_2', 'embedding': [-0.028354483, -0.004594314, -0.027437257, -0.07503127, 0.019797662, -0.01574988, 0.002462638, 0.00081913045, 0.009520535, 0.0018885536, -0.027221126, 0.0044313488, 0.009056382, 0.017124763, 0.1218619, 0.028431928, 0.006889962, 0.008292447, 0.00860764, 0.013960054, 0.031205527, 0.00286837, 0.014437217, -0.0111070955, 0.012287916, -0.03693774, -0.019905472, 0.0011935745, 0.0070624473, -0.0011666402, 0.00081217324, 0.011674519, -0.012037066, 0.028344376, 0.0038506812, 0.018957742, 0.0064415312, -0.025950747, -0.013514697, 0.028753478, -0.0076526357, 0.0043361303, -0.0043254173, 0.03138997, -0.025176778, -0.030117787, 0.026629163, -0.018589241, -0.0033245392, 0.029279923, -0.015315402, 0.005027498, 0.010174726, -0.16073938, 0.0073247016, 0.012884589, 0.0063570947, -0.008419368, 0.02410238, 0.01473813, 0.005207844, 0.006860385, -0.0010170742, -0.009443688, -0.028953496, -0.040927984, 0.012571309, -0.016352229, 0.011467885, -0.011643433, 0.00851476, -0.0057249847, -0.036216006, -0.024202101, 0.008059578, -0.01691831, 0.004474902, 0.016400324, -0.009590895, 0.005715206, -0.014045094, -0.018207183, 0.029093482, 0.00744834, -0.017846322, -0.01655862, -4.6424382e-05, 0.012218396, 0.033705734, 0.00015614623, -0.024872217, -0.026249776, 0.008530209, -0.01021832, 0.0083520105, 0.0052972627, -0.0044145053, 0.010094153, -1.4896408e-05, -0.025411008, -0.034129262, -0.