opensearchpy.helpers.errors.BulkIndexError: ('6 document(s) failed to index.', [{'index': {'_index': 'chunks', '_id': 'chunk_116bfc76-d9ca-4164-b677-253e452eb49a_0', 'status': 400, 'error': {'type': 'mapper_parsing_exception', 'reason': "failed to parse field [embedding] of type [knn_vector] in document with id 'chunk_116bfc76-d9ca-4164-b677-253e452eb49a_0'. Preview of field's value: 'null'", 'caused_by': {'type': 'illegal_argument_exception', 'reason': 'Vector dimension mismatch. Expected: 768, Given: 3072'}}, 'data': {'source_type': <SourceType.UPLOADED_FILE: 'uploaded_file'>, 'source_id': '116bfc76-d9ca-4164-b677-253e452eb49a', 'source_name': 'Bloomberg QRT Article.pdf', 'content': 'The Secretive Hedge Fund Rewriting the Rules of $4.5 Trillion Industry\nMany of finance’s brightest minds have stumbled trying to gatecrash the hedge fund\naristocracy. QRT’s rapid rise from unknown to the top 1% is rewriting the narrative of\nwhat’s possible. By Nishant Kumar\nUnder a low gray sky, a building site sits exposed amid the barren beauty of Iceland in\nwinter. Rising from the frozen earth a timber frame stretches across the view, heralding\nthe birth of a high-spec data center.\nThis isn’t just the usual tale of an AI-giddy tech giant looking to tap an abundant source\nof renewable energy and cooling power to help it crunch colossal amounts of\ninformation. The facility in the northern city of Akureyri is for a secretive hedge fund,\nQube Research & Technologies. And it’s almost certainly a first in this bitterly\ncompetitive $4.5 trillion industry.\nQRT wants the site to give it an edge in the race to find discrepancies in markets, and\nprofit from them — a pursuit that’s already handed it returns that make those of the\nestablished hedge fund elite look mediocre by comparison. But the data-center gambit\nis far from the only thing to set the firm apart.\nLed by former Credit Suisse colleagues Pierre-Yves Morlat, or Pym to his friends, and\nLaurent Laizet, London-based QRT has grown quietly into a $23 billion trading\npowerhouse, all from modest origins as an $800 million spinoff from the Swiss bank just\nseven years ago. The aim is for $30 billion of assets.\nA firm whose name can still be met with blank looks when you mention it to finance\nveterans, it’s now arguably a part of this industry’s aristocracy. Only about 1% of hedge\nfunds in this market manage more than $20 billion, according to estimates by\nresearcher PivotalPath.\nThat gilded group has been starting to resemble a closed shop of late as titans like\nCitadel and Millennium Management use their sheer scale to blow away pretenders,\nand many of finance’s brightest minds have stumbled trying to gatecrash the party. QRT\nis rewriting the narrative of what might be possible.\nIt has no staff in New York where most of the hedge fund big guns reside. It shuns star\nculture and the trigger-happy hiring and firing of employees, sharing out rewards\ninstead. Only one in 10 of its 1,400 workforce is a traditional portfolio manager. Most\nwould fit in just as easily at Google or Meta, and teamwork is prized in the hunt to solve\ncomplex market problems.\nIts fat returns and distinct approach let it charge eye-popping fees of 25% to 30% plus\nadd ons, well above the norm for other “multistrategy” hedge funds, the leviathans who', 'start_pos': None, 'end_pos': None, 'bbox': {'page': 1, 'x': 0.0, 'y': 0.0, 'width': 595.32, 'height': 841.92, 'selector': None, 'xpath': None, 'offset': None, 'line_start': None, 'line_end': None, 'char_start': None, 'char_end': None}, 'metadata': {'page_number': 1, 'page_width': 595.32, 'page_height': 841.92, 'has_embedding': True}, 'id': 'chunk_116bfc76-d9ca-4164-b677-253e452eb49a_0', 'embedding': [-0.020461757, -0.015879737, -0.012274961, -0.08870351, 0.018126972, 0.001289175, 0.015041513, 0.00014554962, 0.011013949, 0.0055155274, -0.01719175, -0.0069788503, 0.021386633, 0.0030234205, 0.11611903, 0.04696649, -0.0060230233, 0.0068418384, 0.020154804