009514841, -0.0043469016, 0.006384802, -0.004579751, -0.00071832864, -0.019022424, 0.012327663, 0.02521529, 0.037916236, -0.013949656, -0.015187476, 0.009925972, 0.002725662, 0.007317775, -0.0068119024, 0.008915224, 0.008004825, 0.02635983, 9.580867e-05, 0.014027256, 0.018458303, 0.010338405, 0.0036737737, -0.028055593, 0.004997928, 0.02185548, 0.009596166, 0.020527828, -0.0025805875, 0.010799124, -0.00523119, 0.015407307, -0.01020801, 0.005395943, -0.01683699, -0.0063777133, -0.0005071407, 0.017517205, -0.0013880068, -0.0072177323, 0.009140475, 0.0056435745, 0.0040167584, -0.007862963, -0.005261583, -0.006573726, -0.0018252607, -0.020911967, -0.017848901, -0.004737567, -0.012211166, 0.011295403, 0.00337059, -0.003986196, -0.006471095, 0.0036754836, -0.0049667563]}}}, {'index': {'_index': 'chunks', '_id': 'chunk_116bfc76-d9ca-4164-b677-253e452eb49a_3', 'status': 400, 'error': {'type': 'mapper_parsing_exception', 'reason': "failed to parse field [embedding] of type [knn_vector] in document with id 'chunk_116bfc76-d9ca-4164-b677-253e452eb49a_3'. Preview of field's value: 'null'", 'caused_by': {'type': 'illegal_argument_exception', 'reason': 'Vector dimension mismatch. Expected: 768, Given: 3072'}}, 'data': {'source_type': <SourceType.UPLOADED_FILE: 'uploaded_file'>, 'source_id': '116bfc76-d9ca-4164-b677-253e452eb49a', 'source_name': 'Bloomberg QRT Article.pdf', 'content': 'some other very talented principals emerge, actually QRT is built around the\norganization.”\nAs with many hedge funds of note, it leans heavily on “quants,” the math prodigies who\ntry to divine market moves. What’s different is the number of techie whiz kids on the\npayroll. It’s spending hundreds of millions of dollars on technology and data, 10 times\nmore than when it spun out from Credit Suisse. Last year it recruited 232 IT staff, 157\nresearchers and just 48 traders.\nSomeone who knows QRT well says it’s as much a tech company as hedge fund.\nAll the quant teams use its core technology and strategy, whose evolution goes back\nyears, to aim for consistency. Getting hired is a saga. But in an industry where careers\ncan be nasty, brutish and short, it has stuck by its people.\nQRT’s first pool of money, the $7 billion multistrat fund Qube, uses computer models to\nevaluate risk, pricing and timing in markets, and then trades at massive volumes. Its\nstrategy has been the founding duo’s main money-spinner since 2003 when they helped\ncreate it at SocGen and then evolved it. Torus, which also trades equities, and Prism,\nwhich includes macro bets and futures, are its other linchpins.\nIts rise has come as investors have withdrawn funds from smaller firms and piled into\nmultistrats. Unable to keep up with demand, the biggest have been mostly closed to\nnew money, forcing backers to go elsewhere. QRT has been a major beneficiary. Torus\nand Qube have also had to stop taking client cash.\nClients have been seduced too by the promise of high “alpha” — jargon for how far its\nreturns beat the herd when adjusted for risk taken — from bets on volatile markets.\nRoughly half its growth in assets comes straight from profits, making it among the most\nprofitable hedge funds.\n“The combination of high alpha and high volatility, not many people do that,” says Marlin\nNaidoo, global head of capital introduction and consulting at BNP Paribas, speaking\ngenerally about hedge funds and not any specific firm.\nStill, the huge leverage and wagers on febrile markets also recall unhappy memories of\npast financial disasters. From Long-Term Capital Management’s collapse to the 2008\nmeltdown, rampant borrowing usually played a part.\nTorus had regulatory assets of $191 billion, according to a US filing in March. Qube\ntraded $121 billion. The latest data isn’t available.\n“All crises that occurred over the last 200 years, it’s always a function of leverage\nbecause somebody else is asking you to return assets,” says Marcus Storr, head of\nalternative investments at €61 billion ($63.4 billion) German hedge fund allocator FERI,\nspeaking generally about the market.', 'start_pos': None, 'end_pos': None, 'bbox': {'page': 4, 'x': 0.0, 'y': 0.0, 'width': 595.32, 'height': 841.92, 'selector': None, 'xpath': None, 'offset': None, 'line_start': None, 'line_end': None, 'char_start': None, 'char_end': None}, 'metadata': {'page_number': 4, 'page_width': 595.32, 'page_height': 841.92, 'has_embedding': True}, 'id': 'chunk_116bfc76-d9ca-4164-b677-253e452eb49a_3', 'embedding': [-0.020538189, -0.010849195, -0.018634787, -0.07803857, 0.013895941, -0.015711365, 0.025785165, -0.00064815953, -0.007746144, 0.028736668, -0.0018140872, 0.00042645037, 0.011991395, 0.013350178, 0.12368358, 0.04026308, 0.00074923004, 0.0027125739, 0.010579184, 0.00021079434, 0.019458888, 0.012440547, -0.006791948, -0.012775978, 0.0043968065, -0.02764318, -0.02004711, 0.00019883455, 0.020928748, -0.0027621286, 0.007725717, 0.004351482, -0.018239243, 0.02176481, -0.0035568103, 0.018315203, 0.007052178, -0.03117523, -0.016085632, 0.036768243, 0.0016672977, 0.0038074108, -0.0071628005, 0.012589455, -0.026390757, -0.033199724, 0.016732275, -0.029879598, 0.0034517974, 0.026475532, -0.020582605, -0.00800036, 0.003282283, -0.16069476, 0.011155676, 0.027995309, 0.00624306, -0.0057168757, 0.025573302, 0.021612288, 0.024553072, 0.01810211, -0.0072168414, -0.0127187185, -0.021911485, -0.042377733, 0.01944802, -0.018480515, -0.002413082, -0.009744971, 0.022782767, 0.003610269, -0.022649301, -0.03276158, -0.006561836, -0.015335098, 0.023391131, 0.01443266, -0.006701325, -0.008264502, 0.0224479, -0.022600114, 0.022009179, -0.003573256, -0.01284203, -0.005675937, -0.010138249, -0.0080246255, 0.037221894, -0.0146080945, 0.0041506337, -0.017874435, 0.010959049, -0.016643943, 0.011515332, -0.007438182, 0.0066318777, 0.034224354, 0.008823693, -0.01575373, -0.014742963, -0.05645516, -0.01733215, -0.00678701, -0.0070336866, -0.003243056, 0.007654168, 0.005354615, 0.00563222, 0.0064568724, 0.035023727,